INSERT INTO `posts` VALUES (1,'auto',NULL,'auto','One weird thing about making creative tools and making music at the same time is that both projects eventually try to eat your entire life.\r\n\r\nYou start with a small idea. Maybe it’s a song that needs a certain sound. Maybe it’s a tool that would “just save a little time.” Then three hours later you’re knee-deep in some ridiculous side path, fixing a problem you didn’t know existed yesterday.\r\n\r\nI’ve learned not to hate that part of the process, even when it’s annoying. A lot of rebuilding happens there. You find the weak spots. You find the lazy assumptions. You find out whether the thing actually works or whether you just liked the idea of it.\r\n\r\nThat applies to music too. Sometimes the version in your head is wrong. Sometimes the first draft is all posture and no weight. Then you strip it down, rebuild it, and somewhere in that mess it starts sounding like something real.\r\n\r\nNot glamorous, but real.\r\n\r\nThat’s probably a big part of what Sonic Foundry is to me. Not pretending the work arrives finished. Not acting like identity drops from the sky fully armored. More like building, testing, failing, fixing, and going again until the thing finally has some truth in it.\r\n\r\nAnd yes, this is also me admitting that “one quick feature” is almost never one quick feature. Developers know. Musicians know. People who make things the hard way definitely know.','draft','2026-08-13 16:47:49','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(2,'behind_the_music','making music through new tools generates a lot of hate from supposed \'gatekeepers\' of art','auto','One thing I’ve noticed pretty quickly: if you make music with new tools, there’s always a small crowd waiting to tell you it doesn’t count.\r\n\r\nNot because they heard the song and hated the song. Not because the writing said nothing. Not because the voice, arrangement, or production failed. Just because you used a tool they already decided is illegitimate.\r\n\r\nThat kind of gatekeeping gets old fast.\r\n\r\nI’m not interested in pretending every new tool is magic, and I’m definitely not interested in handing the whole creative process over to a machine and calling it art. But I’m also not going to act like using new technology somehow erases the hours of writing, revising, testing, discarding bad ideas, rebuilding parts that don’t work, and making a hundred judgment calls that nobody else sees.\r\n\r\nThe tool can help build the sound. It cannot decide who I am, what I’m trying to say, or why a piece should exist in the first place.\r\n\r\nA lot of people talk like suffering through the old way is the only thing that makes the work real. I don’t buy that. Struggle can teach you things. It can also just waste your time. Sometimes the better tool just lets you get to the part that actually matters faster.\r\n\r\nI care about whether a song has weight. I care whether it says something honest. I care whether it sounds like me.\r\n\r\nThe rest is just noise.','draft','2026-08-13 16:51:59','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(3,'auto','just because you dont like the tool, you dont get to say my work isnt art','auto','One thing I’m getting a little tired of is this idea that if someone doesn’t like the tool, they get to dismiss the work entirely.\r\n\r\nYou don’t have to like AI. You don’t have to use it. You don’t have to trust it. That’s all fair game. But “I don’t respect the tool” is not the same thing as “there was no artist involved.”\r\n\r\nA tool can speed things up, open weird doors, and make certain parts of the process less miserable. It can also make a giant mess. Anyone who’s actually tried building something with new tools knows that easier does not automatically mean better. Judgment still matters. Taste still matters. Direction still matters. Knowing when something is wrong matters a lot.\r\n\r\nI’m not interested in handing identity over to a machine. I’m interested in using what helps, rejecting what doesn’t, and making the thing sound like me in the end. That part is still mine. Always was.\r\n\r\nPeople said similar things about recording tech, digital art, sampling, editing, and about a hundred other things once they stopped being new and started being normal.\r\n\r\nYou can argue about tools all day if you want. I’m going to keep making things. And if the work has a voice, intent, shape, and a human hand guiding it, then yeah, it’s art. Even if you don’t like how the hammer looks. test','approved','2026-08-13 17:23:07','2026-08-14 14:14:22','2026-08-13 17:23:07',NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(4,'humor','just because you dont like the tool, you dont get to say my work isnt art','auto','Apparently some people think if they don’t like the tool, they get to disqualify the work.\r\n\r\nThat’s not how this works.\r\n\r\nYou can hate AI-assisted music. You can hate digital art. You can hate samples, synths, drum libraries, plugins, DAWs, editing, photography, video compositing, or anything else that didn’t arrive carved into a stone tablet by the gods themselves.\r\n\r\nStill doesn’t make the work less real.\r\n\r\nA tool is not a replacement for taste, judgment, effort, or identity. It just means the hammer looks different than it used to.\r\n\r\nIf the machine made everyone an artist, every lazy prompt dump would hit like thunder. It doesn’t. Most of it is still just noise. The difference is still the human being making choices.\r\n\r\nYou don’t have to like the tool. That part is easy.\r\n\r\nMaking something worth a damn is the hard part.','rejected','2026-08-13 17:25:38','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(5,'creator_developer','i \'love\' testing new features...NOT','auto','I “love” testing new features.\r\n\r\nBy “love,” I mean I click one button, the app does something deeply cursed, and now I’m staring at my own code like it just insulted my family.\r\n\r\nThis is the part of building stuff people don’t see. You have the cool idea. You build the thing. The thing almost works. Then one tiny change wakes up six completely unrelated problems that were apparently living in the walls the whole time.\r\n\r\nVery noble. Very cinematic. Extremely annoying.\r\n\r\nStill, this is part of the work. Not the shiny part. Not the part anybody writes dramatic music for. Just me, a bug, and the slow realization that my “small feature” has somehow become a whole system again.\r\n\r\nAnyway, back into the mess. If the new feature survives testing, it gets to live. If not, it returns to the pit it came from.','rejected','2026-08-13 17:52:39','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(6,'forge_reflection','i \'love\' testing new features...NOT','auto','I “love” testing new features.\r\n\r\nYou build one little thing because it seems useful. Nice and simple. Clean idea. Shouldn’t take long.\r\n\r\nThen you start testing it and immediately find out it has somehow grown eight hidden heads, three bad assumptions, and one deeply personal grudge against working correctly.\r\n\r\nThere’s a very specific kind of humility in watching your clever idea break in ways that never even occurred to you while you were building it. You go in feeling competent and come out muttering at your screen like a medieval peasant accusing the weather of witchcraft.\r\n\r\nStill, this is part of the work. Not the glamorous part, obviously. Nobody writes songs about edge cases and broken workflows. But this is where a lot of real building happens. You find the weak spots. You fix what you can. You rebuild the part that was apparently held together by optimism and bad timing.\r\n\r\nSo yes, I “love” testing new features.\r\n\r\nIn the same way I “love” discovering that the quick fix has become a full evening project.','approved','2026-08-13 17:53:00','2026-08-14 14:14:22','2026-08-13 17:53:00',NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(7,'behind_the_music','I loved the idea of building Sonic Foundry.  now how do i market it?','auto','Building Sonic Foundry was the fun part.\r\n\r\nThat part makes sense to me. You get an idea, start pulling pieces together, solve one problem, accidentally create three more, and somehow end up with an actual thing. That’s my natural habitat.\r\n\r\nMarketing it is a completely different beast.\r\n\r\nWriting code, building features, shaping tools for music creation... I can do that all day. But the second the question becomes “okay, now explain this clearly, put it in front of people, and don’t sound like a robot or a late-night infomercial,” my brain starts looking for the nearest exit.\r\n\r\nI know a lot of creators and developers hit that wall. Making the thing feels honest. Talking about the thing can feel weird as hell, especially when you actually care about it and don’t want to turn it into generic hype.\r\n\r\nSo that’s kind of where I’m at with Sonic Foundry right now. Built with a lot of excitement. Built because I wanted it to exist. Now I’m figuring out how to tell that story without making it sound like “disruptive AI-powered revolutionary synergy for creators,” because I would rather walk into traffic.\r\n\r\nHonestly, part of the answer is probably just showing the work more. What it does. Why I built it. What problems it solves. What weird ideas led to it in the first place.\r\n\r\nTurns out building the machine and learning how to talk about the machine are two different skills.\r\n\r\nIf you’ve ever made something you believed in, did the marketing part come naturally to you, or did you have to learn it the hard way too?','draft','2026-08-13 18:00:50','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(8,'auto',NULL,'auto','One of the stranger parts of making music now is that the tools keep changing faster than your taste does.\r\n\r\nYou can learn a new workflow, build some weird little system to speed things up, get excited because now you can sketch ideas faster, and then realize speed does not actually answer the hard question. It just gets you to the hard question sooner.\r\n\r\nDoes this part sound like me or not?\r\n\r\nThat’s still the job. Not just making noise. Not just finishing tracks. Figuring out what belongs in the House and what doesn’t.\r\n\r\nI like the idea of using whatever tools help me build bigger, heavier, stranger things. I do not like the idea of every artist getting pushed through the same machine until all the rough edges disappear. The rough edges are usually the part worth keeping.\r\n\r\nA lot of this project has been learning how to use new tools without handing them the keys.\r\n\r\nThat part matters to me. Probably more than the shiny part.\r\n\r\nIf you make things, music or otherwise, have you run into that too? The point where the tool got easier, but the real decision stayed just as hard?','draft','2026-08-13 18:16:22','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(9,'auto',NULL,'auto','One of the weird parts of making music now is that the tools keep changing faster than your habits do.\r\n\r\nYou finally get comfortable. You know your workflow, your shortcuts, your little tricks, the exact dumb ritual that gets you from blank project to something that actually sounds like a song. Then a new tool shows up, or a new way of building something, and suddenly you\'re back to being the guy poking at buttons like a confused blacksmith who just got handed a laptop.\r\n\r\nI don\'t hate that, honestly. It\'s annoying, but it\'s useful. Starting over a little bit keeps you honest. It reminds you that making the thing still matters more than protecting your ego.\r\n\r\nThat\'s part of House Dainislaav for me. Not pretending you\'ve arrived. Not acting like experience means you never have to be clumsy again. Just keeping your hands in the work and being willing to suck at a new part of it until it becomes yours.\r\n\r\nSome of the best things I\'ve made came after the point where I was pretty sure I had no idea what I was doing. Which, to be fair, is still a recurring theme.','draft','2026-08-13 18:16:36','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(10,'humor',NULL,'auto','Every House Dainislaav project starts the same way:\r\n\r\n“I’m just going to make one quick thing.”\r\n\r\nThen three hours later I’m knee-deep in audio layers, visual ideas, a tool I accidentally started building for myself, and some note in the middle of it all that just says “make it heavier somehow.”\r\n\r\nThis is how a simple creative idea turns into music, software, artwork, and at least one minor identity crisis.\r\n\r\nAnyway, if you’ve ever opened your DAW, your code editor, or your gear bin for “a few minutes” and resurfaced in another timeline, welcome to the House.','draft','2026-08-13 18:17:12','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(11,'humor',NULL,'auto','Every creative project starts the same way.\r\n\r\n“I’m just going to make one small thing.”\r\n\r\nThen three hours later I’m naming folders like FINAL_final_real_thisone, rewriting a section that was perfectly fine yesterday, and explaining to myself why this absolutely requires new visuals, new layers, and a completely unnecessary side system that did not exist when I woke up.\r\n\r\nMusicians, developers, and anyone who has ever touched a “quick project” know exactly what I’m talking about.\r\n\r\nHouse Dainislaav may run on discipline, but it is also powered by “well, since I’m already here, I might as well rebuild the whole damn thing.”','draft','2026-08-13 18:17:47','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(12,'mythic_adventures','Leading the house as Named Heir isnt as glorious as it sounds','auto','Being Named Heir sounds impressive right up until you realize it mostly means responsibility with better lighting.\r\n\r\nPeople tend to picture the dramatic parts. The banner. The title. The big moments where everyone looks to you and you try very hard to look like you absolutely meant to stand that way. What they don’t always see is the rest of it. Keeping track of people. Making decisions when none of the options feel great. Being the one who has to stay steady when things get messy, tired, frustrating, or unclear.\r\n\r\nA leadership title in game still comes with a very real kind of weight. Even in a mythic world with armor, banners, and all the rest, you still end up dealing with trust, responsibility, loyalty, and the fact that other people are counting on you to not disappear into the bushes when things get complicated.\r\n\r\nThere’s good stuff in it too, obviously. Pride. Purpose. The feeling that what you do matters to more than just you. But “glorious” is only part of the picture. A lot of it is quieter than that. More work. More pressure. More moments where you have to choose between being comfortable and being useful.\r\n\r\nStill worth it.\r\n\r\nJust not quite as cinematic as people think. Until it is.','draft','2026-08-13 18:19:21','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(13,'humor','Leading the house as Named Heir isnt as glorious as it sounds','auto','Being the Named Heir sounds dramatic until you realize a lot of it is just answering questions, carrying too much responsibility at once, and trying to look like you absolutely meant to make that decision.\r\n\r\nPeople hear “Named Heir” and picture banners, power, and grim speeches on a windy hill.\r\n\r\nWhat they don’t picture is the part where you’re tired, half your plans are on fire, three people need something from you at the same time, and you still have to act like this was all part of the grand design.\r\n\r\nNobility is fake.\r\nAdministration is real.','draft','2026-08-13 18:20:02','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(14,'humor','Leading the house as Named Heir isnt as glorious as it sounds','auto','Being Named Heir sounds very dramatic until you realize half the job is trying to keep the House moving while also answering 47 questions that begin with, “Hey, real quick…”\r\n\r\nVery noble. Very legendary. Very much unpaid fantasy middle management.\r\n\r\nFrom a distance it looks like banners, command, legacy, and solemn responsibility.\r\n\r\nUp close it’s logistics, problem solving, and wondering how one group of capable adults became completely unable to make a simple decision the second you sat down.\r\n\r\nStill love it, obviously. But some of the most powerful leadership moments in any House are basically:\r\n“Did anyone bring the thing?”\r\n“Who has the other thing?”\r\n“Why is that on fire?”\r\n“No, not like that.”\r\n\r\nThat’s the job.','approved','2026-08-13 18:20:39','2026-08-14 14:14:22','2026-08-13 18:20:39',NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(15,'forge_reflection','Leading the house as Named Heir isnt as glorious as it sounds','auto','Being Named Heir sounds a lot cooler than a lot of it actually feels.\r\n\r\nPeople hear a title like that and picture banners, big speeches, command, respect, all the dramatic parts. What they usually don’t picture is responsibility, pressure, second-guessing yourself, and the quiet little voice asking whether you’re carrying it well enough for the people who trusted you with it.\r\n\r\nA lot of leadership is less “glory” and more showing up tired, paying attention, keeping your word, and trying to make decisions you can live with later. Sometimes it means being steady when you’d rather just go be loud somewhere and hit things with a sword like a normal person.\r\n\r\nI’m learning that carrying a house isn’t about looking impressive. It’s about being dependable. Not perfect. Not fearless. Just dependable, especially on the days when the title feels heavier than it sounds.','draft','2026-08-13 18:21:57','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(16,'forge_reflection',NULL,'auto','I’ve been thinking about how annoying it is to become a beginner again.\r\n\r\nYou spend years getting decent at one thing, and somewhere along the way you start expecting that competence to carry over into everything else. Then you try something new and suddenly you’re fumbling around like an idiot, making avoidable mistakes, looking up basic stuff, fixing problems you created five minutes earlier.\r\n\r\nThat part stings more than most people admit.\r\n\r\nBut I’m starting to think that starting over is part of the work. Not the glamorous part. Not the part anybody brags about. I mean the part where your pride gets dragged behind the cart for a while and you keep going anyway because something in you knows you’re not supposed to stay where you already know how to function.\r\n\r\nThere’s a kind of honesty in that.\r\n\r\nYou find out pretty fast whether you actually care about the thing, because once the novelty wears off, all that’s left is repetition, frustration, and the choice to come back tomorrow and do it a little better than you did today.\r\n\r\nI don’t think becoming is usually dramatic. Most of the time it looks like bad first drafts, clumsy attempts, reworked plans, and a stubborn refusal to quit just because your ego had a rough afternoon.\r\n\r\nThat’s probably a better teacher than comfort ever was.','draft','2026-08-13 18:22:48','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(17,'forge_reflection',NULL,'auto','I’ve had to learn, more than once, that starting over in one part of your life does not care how competent you were in another.\r\n\r\nYou can spend years getting good at one thing, build habits, build confidence, get comfortable being the guy who knows what he’s doing… and then you step into something new and suddenly you’re an idiot again. Not in some poetic, beautiful way either. Just plain old “why is this not working” frustration.\r\n\r\nThat’s been on my mind lately. A lot of becoming is less glamorous than people make it sound. Sometimes it’s not a big breakthrough. Sometimes it’s sitting there, doing bad work until it becomes less bad, and trying not to romanticize the process while you’re in the middle of it.\r\n\r\nI used to think progress would eventually make that feeling go away. It doesn’t. You just get a little less afraid of it. You learn that being a beginner again is not proof that you’ve failed. It usually means you’re still willing to move.\r\n\r\nThere’s something honest about that. No title, no image, no past version of yourself gets to swing the hammer for you. If the work matters, you do it anyway. Even clumsy. Even annoyed. Even on the days when nothing comes together cleanly.\r\n\r\nThat’s not a glorious answer, but it’s a real one.','rejected','2026-08-13 18:23:12','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(18,'forge_reflection',NULL,'auto','I’ve been thinking about the unglamorous part of making anything worth keeping.\r\n\r\nNot the exciting part where the idea first hits and everything feels big and alive. Not the part where you can already see the finished thing in your head and it looks amazing because reality hasn’t touched it yet. I mean the middle. The slow part. The part where you’ve seen the flaws, the novelty is gone, and now the work is mostly showing back up to deal with what’s actually there.\r\n\r\nThat part is a lot less cinematic than people like to pretend. A lot of it is fixing your own bad decisions. A lot of it is admitting the first version wasn’t as brilliant as you thought it was at 1:30 in the morning. A lot of it is doing the same kind of careful, repetitive work that nobody claps for because it doesn’t look dramatic from the outside.\r\n\r\nBut I trust that stage more than the rush.\r\n\r\nAnybody can love the beginning of something. The beginning is flattering. The middle is where you find out whether you actually care enough to keep shaping it when it stops being exciting and starts being honest.\r\n\r\nThat goes for songs, projects, promises, and probably a few parts of life I would have preferred to learn in an easier way.','draft','2026-08-13 18:29:21','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(19,'forge_reflection',NULL,'auto','I’ve been thinking about the difference between working hard and actually making progress.\r\n\r\nThey are not always the same thing, which is annoying, because “I am clearly suffering here” feels like it should count for more than it does.\r\n\r\nIt’s easy to spend a lot of time pushing on the wrong part of a problem. Reworking the same section. Tweaking the same idea. Hitting something with more effort because admitting it needs a different approach feels a little too much like failure. Sometimes the most stubborn part of the process is not the task. It’s your pride.\r\n\r\nI don’t mean that in some grand dramatic way. I mean the very ordinary kind. The kind that says, “I’ve already put this much time into it, so now I have to make this version work.” Even when the smarter move is to cut the thing apart, rethink it, ask for help, or admit the plan was off.\r\n\r\nThere’s a kind of discipline in staying with difficult work. There’s another kind in noticing when you’re just grinding your gears and calling it commitment.\r\n\r\nI’m still learning the difference.','draft','2026-08-13 18:30:10','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(20,'forge_reflection',NULL,'auto','I’ve been thinking about the point where “I can handle it myself” stops being strength and starts being stupidity.\r\n\r\nThere’s a certain kind of pride that feels useful because it looks like discipline from the outside. You keep pushing, keep troubleshooting, keep carrying the thing, keep telling yourself you’ll sort it out alone because that’s what competent people do. And sometimes that is the job.\r\n\r\nBut sometimes you’re not being solid. You’re just being stubborn in a way that wastes time and wears you down.\r\n\r\nI don’t mean needing applause or constant reassurance. I mean the ordinary human fact that sometimes another set of eyes sees the problem faster. Sometimes somebody else knows the tool, the process, or the blind spot you keep circling for three hours like an idiot. I have definitely done this. More than once. Probably more than I should admit in public.\r\n\r\nThere’s a difference between earning your strength and making a religion out of unnecessary struggle.\r\n\r\nStill learning that one.','draft','2026-08-13 18:31:02','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(21,'behind_the_music',NULL,'auto','One thing I enjoy more than I probably should is the moment a song stops being a cool idea and starts becoming a problem.\r\n\r\nAt the beginning, everything sounds great in your head. Big drums. Heavy guitars. Low vocals. Atmosphere. Drama. You can already hear the finished thing like some glorious lie your brain is telling you. Then you actually open the project and now it’s 37 tracks, two parts are fighting each other, the chorus lost half its impact, and something that sounded massive yesterday suddenly sounds like a wet cardboard wall today.\r\n\r\nThat part is annoying, but it’s also where the real work starts. Not the dramatic inspired part. The judgment part. Figuring out what actually serves the song and what’s just there because I got carried away and liked the noise. Sometimes the best thing I can do for a track is stop adding and start cutting.\r\n\r\nA lot of the House sound comes from that process. Not just building things up, but stripping away the stuff that looks impressive and keeping the parts that actually hit. Weight matters more when it has space around it.\r\n\r\nThat’s true in music more often than people think. “More” is very easy. Better is harder.','draft','2026-08-13 18:33:45','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(22,'forge_reflection',NULL,'auto','I’ve been thinking about the difference between making something better and just refusing to stop touching it.\r\n\r\nThere’s a point in creative work where fixing things is useful, and then there’s the part where you’re just circling the same ground because finishing it means you have to let it stand on its own. That part is a lot less noble than people make it sound. Sometimes it isn’t craftsmanship. Sometimes it’s nerves wearing a very respectable hat.\r\n\r\nI’m not talking about rushing something. Some things really do need more time, more thought, more work. But I’ve had to learn that “not done yet” can become a hiding place if you let it. You keep adjusting, polishing, reworking, because as long as it stays in progress, it can still be perfect in your head.\r\n\r\nOnce it’s finished, it has to be real. Real means uneven in places. Real means you made choices and now you have to live with them. Real means somebody else gets to hear it, see it, or judge it without access to all the versions that almost existed.\r\n\r\nThat’s uncomfortable, but I’m starting to think that discomfort is part of the job. Not every piece of work needs one more pass. Sometimes it needs your nerve more than your perfectionism.','draft','2026-08-13 18:34:53','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(23,'forge_reflection',NULL,'auto','I’ve been thinking about the difference between being interested in something and being responsible for it.\r\n\r\nA lot of things are fun when they’re still optional. You can enjoy the idea, talk about the idea, make plans, collect tools, imagine where it could go. That part feels good. It still belongs to possibility, which is a very comfortable place to live.\r\n\r\nResponsibility feels different. Responsibility is showing up for the thing when it has become ordinary. When it needs maintenance. When it’s inconvenient. When you’re tired. When the exciting part burned off and now it’s just yours to carry.\r\n\r\nI’ve learned those are not the same trait at all.\r\n\r\nIt’s easy to mistake enthusiasm for commitment when everything is still new. The real test usually shows up later, when there’s no mood helping you and no big dramatic moment around it. Just the quiet question of whether you’re still going to do what you said you were going to do.\r\n\r\nThat applies to creative work, leadership, promises, all of it.\r\n\r\nA lot of identity gets built there, honestly. Not in the loud moments. In the boring ones. In the part where nobody claps and the work still needs doing.','draft','2026-08-13 18:36:13','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(24,'humor',NULL,'auto','House Dainislaav is, at least in theory, a music project.\r\n\r\nIn practice, it is also 14 unfinished ideas, 9 rendered video tests, 3 versions of the same artwork, a folder of “final_final_real_final” files, and one completely unnecessary side quest I absolutely created for myself.\r\n\r\nEvery creative project starts with, “I’m just going to make this one thing.”\r\n\r\nThen five hours later you’re learning something you did not need to know, fixing a problem you invented, and seriously considering building a whole new system because the old one annoyed you once.\r\n\r\nThis is not chaos.\r\nThis is apparently my process.','rejected','2026-08-13 18:36:42','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(25,'humor',NULL,'auto','Every music project has that one innocent little task that turns into a full archaeological dig.\r\n\r\n“I’m just going to clean up this vocal track.”\r\n\r\nThree hours later I’m reopening old plugin chains, renaming files from 2023, questioning a mix decision made by a previous version of myself, and finding a folder that might as well be labeled: HERE LIES BAD JUDGMENT.\r\n\r\nThis happens in development too, which is probably why my brain thinks this is normal. You go in to fix one thing and come back out carrying six unrelated problems, two new ideas, and a strong suspicion that past-you was both ambitious and unsupervised.\r\n\r\nCreative work is fun because sometimes you get to make something powerful.\r\n\r\nCreative work is also dumb because sometimes the real quest is figuring out why “final_v2_actual” exists next to “final_v2_actual_USE_THIS_ONE”.','draft','2026-08-13 18:37:44','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(26,'humor',NULL,'auto','LARP has a very specific kind of confidence.\r\n\r\nYou’ll be standing in a field in partial armor, holding a weapon, discussing tactics like the fate of kingdoms hangs in the balance...\r\n\r\nand then somebody’s belt pouch explodes, a spoon falls out, two people forget what side they’re on for a second, and someone in the background is asking if anyone brought extra safety pins.\r\n\r\nThat’s one of the reasons I love it, honestly.\r\n\r\nFrom ten feet away: grim resolve, banners, steel, destiny.\r\n\r\nFrom up close: “Wait, is this yours?” and three grown adults trying to fix a costume failure like it’s a battlefield emergency.\r\n\r\nMajestic. Glorious. Held together by leather straps and collective improvisation.','rejected','2026-08-13 18:54:51','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(27,'humor',NULL,'auto','There’s a very specific kind of musician optimism that says, “I’ll just record one quick vocal take before bed.”\r\n\r\nThat sentence has never once told the truth.\r\n\r\nNow it’s 1:17 a.m., I’m standing in front of a mic repeating the same line 19 times because take 7 had the right attitude, take 11 had the right pronunciation, take 14 had a weird mouth noise, and take 19 somehow sounded like I was announcing the fall of an empire from inside a blanket.\r\n\r\nThen comes the truly dignified part: zooming all the way in on the waveform like I’m a forensic investigator trying to solve a crime committed by my own face.\r\n\r\nCreative work is very noble and mythic until you’re cutting out lip smacks one by one in total silence.','rejected','2026-08-13 18:55:37','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(28,'auto','a humorous joke about using AI to generate music','auto','Using AI to help make music has created a very specific modern problem.\r\n\r\nYou ask the machine for something simple like “dark cinematic metal with weight and atmosphere,” and for one brief, delusional moment you think this is going to save time.\r\n\r\nThen it gives you 14 seconds of something that sounds like a haunted gym trailer, one beautiful transition you wish you wrote first, a choir that seems deeply committed to the wrong emotion, and drums that act like they’ve never met a human being.\r\n\r\nSo now, instead of skipping the work, you’ve unlocked a new kind of work. You’re not replacing songwriting. You’re sorting through robot mischief with editor brain, producer brain, and occasionally the exhausted expression of a man trying to explain to a machine that “more epic” is not actually a useful personality trait.\r\n\r\nAI is a tool. A weird one. Sometimes helpful, sometimes drunk on its own nonsense.\r\n\r\nBut when it does hand you one piece that actually fits, I’m not going to lie, that’s a fun moment.','approved','2026-08-13 19:41:30','2026-08-14 14:14:22','2026-08-13 19:41:30',NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(29,'engagement','a humorous joke about using AI to generate music','auto','Using AI to help make music has created a very specific kind of nonsense.\r\n\r\nYou ask for something simple, and the machine responds with 12 seconds of greatness, 40 seconds of confusion, a choir that sounds like it’s summoning a tax audit, and one transition so good it immediately becomes your problem.\r\n\r\nThat’s the part people don’t see. AI does not remove the work. It just changes the shape of the work. Now instead of staring at a blank project, you’re sifting through beautiful madness looking for the parts that actually belong in the song.\r\n\r\nAnd sometimes, annoyingly, the machine stumbles into something that makes you stop and go, “Okay... that was rude.”\r\n\r\nWhat’s the weirdest, funniest, or most unexpectedly useful thing you’ve gotten out of an AI music tool?','approved','2026-08-13 19:43:05','2026-08-14 14:14:22','2026-08-13 19:43:05',NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(30,'auto','Testing Foundry Herald Facebook publishing. This post will be removed.','auto','Quick test of the Foundry Herald publishing flow.\r\n\r\nIf you’re seeing this, the machinery is working, the ravens are flying, and I have successfully taught one more system to shout into Facebook on my behalf.\r\n\r\nThis post is just a test and will be removed shortly.','published','2026-08-13 19:47:26','2026-08-14 14:14:22','2026-08-13 19:47:26','2026-08-13 19:47:35','997458080122297_122122735688922230',1,NULL),(31,'auto','Second round of testing Foundry Herald Facebook publishing. This post will be removed.','auto','Second round of testing the Foundry Herald publishing flow.\r\n\r\nIf you’re seeing this, the system is alive, the gears are turning, and I have once again convinced a machine to carry a message into Facebook without wandering off into the woods.\r\n\r\nThis is just a test post, and it’ll be removed after I confirm everything is behaving itself.','approved','2026-08-13 20:15:38','2026-08-14 14:14:22','2026-08-13 20:15:38',NULL,NULL,1,'Only approved posts can be published.'),(32,'auto','third (and hopefully, final) round of testing Foundry Herald Facebook publishing. This post will be removed.','auto','Third round of testing the Foundry Herald Facebook publishing flow.\r\n\r\nIf you’re seeing this, the system is still alive, the message got through, and I have once again allowed software to wander into Facebook carrying my name on a banner. This is hopefully the final test before I stop poking the machinery with a stick.\r\n\r\nThis post will be removed after I confirm everything behaves itself.','approved','2026-08-13 20:20:04','2026-08-14 14:14:22','2026-08-13 20:20:04',NULL,NULL,1,'Only approved posts can be published.'),(33,'auto','a welcome message to all who follow the page.  while this page has shorts of the music i create, there\'s more to House Dainislaav than just music.  for full more music, head on over to my youtube channel, https://www.youtube.com/@DainislaavTheIronVoice','auto','Welcome to House Dainislaav.\r\n\r\nIf you’re following this page because of the shorts, the music, the voice, the strange mix of dark banners, heavy sound, and whatever else I’ve built here over time, I’m glad you’re here.\r\n\r\nThis page isn’t just a place where I throw music clips into the void. House Dainislaav is bigger than that. It’s the songs, yes, but it’s also the ideas behind them, the work that goes into them, the experiments, the visuals, the process, the failures, the rebuilding, and the whole ongoing mess of making something that actually feels like it has a soul.\r\n\r\nIf you want the full tracks and more of the music itself, head over to my YouTube channel:\r\nhttps://www.youtube.com/@DainislaavTheIronVoice\r\n\r\nMore will be shared here too, but I wanted to say this clearly: if you’re here, welcome. This House was built piece by piece, and I appreciate every person who’s taken the road in.','approved','2026-08-13 20:30:46','2026-08-14 14:14:22','2026-08-13 20:30:46',NULL,NULL,1,'Only approved posts can be published.'),(34,'creator_developer','creativity is my problem...i have loads of that.  its remembering what i\'ve already posted so i\'m not covering the same ground again for the 3rd time.  now i have a tool for that.  The Herald.  it remembers for me.  Now, i can create content, store it, and if i start writing on the same topic, it lets me know.  and once i have my content the way i want it, i push it right from the tool.  no copy/paste into facebook.  my app sends it','auto','Creativity has never really been the bottleneck.\r\n\r\nThe actual problem is remembering what I already said two weeks ago so I don’t come back around and make the same post again with slightly different armor on it.\r\n\r\nThat is, apparently, a problem I solved the obvious way: by building software.\r\n\r\nSo now The Herald keeps track of what I’ve written, stores the content, warns me when I’m wandering back into the same ground again, and lets me send the post directly once it’s ready. No copy/paste ritual. No digging through old posts like a confused archivist in my own house. I write it, review it, and the app sends it.\r\n\r\nThis is the most predictable version of me possible. I had a content problem, so naturally I made myself another system.\r\n\r\nHonestly though, this kind of thing matters to me. Not because posting needs to feel automated, but because I want more room for the part that actually matters: making the work, shaping the voice, and not repeating myself by accident like some kind of haunted echo chamber with a keyboard.\r\n\r\nThe machine can remember the archive.\r\nI’ll handle the creating part.','draft','2026-08-13 21:07:10','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(35,'creator_developer','creativity isnt my problem...i have loads of that.  its remembering what i\'ve already posted so i\'m not covering the same ground again for the 3rd time.  now i have a tool for that.  The Herald.  it remembers for me.  Now, i can create content, store it, and if i start writing on the same topic, it lets me know.  and once i have my content the way i want it, i push it right from the tool.  no copy/paste into facebook.  my app sends it','auto','Creativity has never really been the bottleneck. I’ve got plenty of ideas. The actual problem is remembering what I already said last week so I don’t come back around and make the same post again wearing slightly different armor.\r\n\r\nSo naturally, I handled that the reasonable way: I built a tool.\r\n\r\nThe Herald keeps track of the content I’ve written, stores drafts, warns me when I’m drifting back into the same topic again, and lets me finish and send the post straight to Facebook from inside the app. No copy/paste. No digging through old posts trying to figure out whether I already made this exact point three times.\r\n\r\nIt’s a very developer solution to a very creator problem.\r\n\r\nHonestly, this is how a lot of my projects happen. I start with one small annoyance, tell myself I’m just fixing a simple workflow issue, and then a whole system appears because apparently I don’t know how to leave anything alone.\r\n\r\nStill, this one’s useful. It clears out some of the mental clutter and lets me spend more time actually making things instead of trying to remember whether I already made the same joke, same observation, or same point in a darker color palette.','approved','2026-08-13 21:30:16','2026-08-14 14:14:22','2026-08-13 21:30:16',NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(36,'humor','So, I accidentally created a social media content generator... (make no mention of AI)','auto','So, I accidentally created a social media content generator.\r\n\r\nThis was not the plan.\r\n\r\nThe plan was “make one useful tool so I can stop juggling drafts, notes, half-posts, and the increasingly stupid system of reminding myself what I already said.” A perfectly normal, contained little project.\r\n\r\nThen, as these things do, it grew legs.\r\n\r\nNow it stores posts, helps me sort ideas, keeps me from repeating myself, and basically stands there like, “No, you already made that joke in different armor three weeks ago.”\r\n\r\nSo yes. I sat down to solve one annoying problem and somehow ended up building the thing that now helps me write content for House Dainislaav.\r\n\r\nThis is why developers are dangerous.\r\n\r\nWe do not build “a small helper.”\r\nWe build a doorway into a new maintenance responsibility and then act surprised when it has a name.','draft','2026-08-13 21:40:34','2026-08-14 14:14:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(37,'humor','I\'m not unemployed...I\'m an unfunded SaaS startup!','auto','I’m not unemployed.\r\n\r\nI’m an unfunded SaaS startup.\r\n\r\nRevenue: none.\r\nVision: enormous.\r\nCurrent strategy: build one more feature and somehow become profitable through force of will, caffeine, and increasingly questionable late-night decisions.\r\n\r\nSome people are “job hunting.”\r\nI am apparently in pre-launch.\r\n\r\nIf you’ve ever built software, made music, or created anything from scratch, you already know this stage. Half the battle is the work. The other half is trying to explain to normal people that yes, technically I was productive today, even if all I have to show for it is a bug fix, a better workflow, and three new problems I created myself.\r\n\r\nHonestly, this may be the most accurate title I’ve had in a while.','approved','2026-08-14 10:55:28','2026-08-14 14:14:22','2026-08-14 10:55:28',NULL,NULL,1,'Error validating access token: Session has expired on Thursday, 13-Aug-26 18:00:00 PDT. The current time is Friday, 14-Aug-26 07:55:54 PDT.'),(38,'behind_the_music','Guard the First Ember','auto','“Guard the First Ember” came out of that feeling where something small still matters, even when everything bigger around it feels like a mess.\r\n\r\nNot a grand victory. Not the final charge. Just the decision to keep one important thing alive.\r\n\r\nThat’s part of what I like about writing in the House Dainislaav world. Sometimes the image is huge and cinematic, and sometimes the whole song turns on one simple idea: protect the thing that lets everything else begin again.\r\n\r\nA lot of the heavier House material leans into impact, weight, movement, all of that. This one still belongs to that world, but the center of it is more careful. More deliberate. Less “burn it all down,” more “make sure the flame doesn’t go out while the storm is doing its best.”\r\n\r\nThat mood changes how I hear everything around the song too. The vocal delivery, the pacing, the space in the arrangement. If the core idea is guarding something fragile, the music has to carry that tension without getting soft and forgetting what page it’s on.\r\n\r\nThat balance is always the interesting part. Heavy, but not mindless. Defiant, but not just noise.\r\n\r\nThat’s where “Guard the First Ember” lives.','published','2026-08-14 13:05:41','2026-08-14 14:43:53','2026-08-14 14:41:12','2026-08-14 14:43:53','997458080122297_122122855064922230',1,NULL),(39,'auto','we\'ve passed the 300 followers milestone','auto','We crossed 300 followers.\r\n\r\nThat may not sound huge in internet terms, but I’m not interested in pretending every number needs to be framed like world conquest. For a project built piece by piece, song by song, strange idea by strange idea, it matters to me.\r\n\r\nSo thank you for being here. Thank you for listening, watching, commenting, sharing, and generally encouraging this whole House Dainislaav thing to keep growing into whatever it’s becoming.\r\n\r\nI appreciate every one of you who decided this dark, loud, stubborn corner of the internet was worth sticking around for.\r\n\r\nMore music. More experiments. More House stuff ahead.','approved','2026-08-14 13:33:31','2026-08-14 14:14:22','2026-08-14 13:33:31',NULL,NULL,1,'FoundryHerald\\Services\\FacebookPublisher::publishText(): Argument #1 ($destination) must be of type array, string given, called in D:\\forge-herald\\public\\api\\publish-post.php on line 82'),(40,'auto','so, we gained more than 300 followers.  now what?','auto','So apparently more than 300 of you are here now, which is both awesome and a little surreal.\r\n\r\nIf you followed for the music, good. That’s the heart of this whole thing. If you followed for the darker medieval visuals, the Iron Voice, the weird overlap between songs, stories, code, and creative experiments... also good. That mess is all part of the same house.\r\n\r\nSo what now?\r\n\r\nNow I keep building.\r\n\r\nMore music. More videos. More behind-the-scenes stuff. More of the process, including the parts that work and the parts that absolutely do not. House Dainislaav was never meant to be some polished fake persona standing on a mountain pretending to have all the answers. It’s work. It’s trial and error. It’s making something real and seeing who it speaks to.\r\n\r\nAnd honestly, I’d like to hear from you too. What made you follow in the first place? The music? The visuals? The House identity? The creator/process side of things? Something else entirely?\r\n\r\nI’m glad you’re here. Let’s see what this becomes.','approved','2026-08-14 13:42:05','2026-08-14 13:48:48','2026-08-14 13:44:07',NULL,NULL,1,'FoundryHerald\\Services\\FacebookPublisher::publishText(): Argument #1 ($destination) must be of type array, null given, called in D:\\forge-herald\\public\\api\\publish-post.php on line 84'),(41,'humor','Testing yet another feature.  Adding brand recognition to the content generation engine','auto','I added “brand recognition” to the content generation engine, which is developer language for:\r\n\r\n“I touched one small thing and now there are six new settings, three edge cases, and at least one decision that should probably count as philosophy.”\r\n\r\nThe good news is the machine is getting better at recognizing House Dainislaav.\r\n\r\nThe bad news is I now have to define, in software, the difference between “cinematic” and “trying way too hard.”\r\n\r\nThis is how projects become kingdoms. You start with one practical feature and a few hours later you’re naming systems like they’ll one day be feared by your enemies.','approved','2026-08-14 14:10:03','2026-08-14 14:40:45','2026-08-14 14:10:03',NULL,NULL,1,'Only approved posts can be published.'),(42,'humor','testing the branding feature.  this is a test and will be removed from the feed','auto','Testing the branding feature means I now get to experience one of software development’s oldest traditions:\r\n\r\nbuilding a tool to make things look more intentional while the test post itself looks like a hostage note.\r\n\r\nThis is a test. It will be removed from the feed.\r\n\r\nIf it somehow becomes the most organized thing posted here all week, that would be deeply on-brand for development.','published','2026-08-14 16:09:22','2026-08-14 16:22:17','2026-08-14 16:22:05','2026-08-14 16:22:17','1237870646072318_122112573765396744',2,NULL),(43,'auto','I spent all day building a feature that should have taken an hour.','auto','I spent all day building a feature that, in my head, was an \"easy one-hour job.\"\r\n\r\nYou know the kind. Add one little improvement, wire it into the workflow, done by lunch, back to the more important stuff.\r\n\r\nInstead, it turned into the usual chain reaction:\r\nthe feature touched project state,\r\nwhich touched decision history,\r\nwhich touched how creative context gets passed forward,\r\nwhich meant I had to stop and ask whether I was fixing a small annoyance or exposing a deeper design problem.\r\n\r\nThat’s the part people don’t always see in creative software.\r\n\r\nA lot of the work is not the button. It’s making sure the button actually means something in the larger process. If Sonic Foundry is supposed to help artists make deliberate choices, then even small features can’t just be slapped on top. They have to fit the logic of how a song, project, or identity develops over time.\r\n\r\nSo yes, the one-hour feature ate the day.\r\n\r\nAnnoying? A little.\r\n\r\nUseful? Definitely.\r\n\r\nBecause those are often the days where the product gets less flashy and more real.','published','2026-08-14 16:12:29','2026-08-14 16:12:39','2026-08-14 16:12:29','2026-08-14 16:12:39','1237870646072318_122112572457396744',2,NULL),(44,'creator_developer','so, i now have a fully brand aware content generator\r\nFoundry Herald: a multi-brand content generation and publishing agent.  this is not specific to House Dainislaav','auto','So, I’ve reached that dangerous stage of building software where the tool now understands brands.\r\n\r\nFoundry Herald started as a way to help me generate, organize, and publish content without losing track of what I’ve already said. Perfectly reasonable. Nice and contained. Very responsible.\r\n\r\nIt is no longer contained.\r\n\r\nNow it’s a multi-brand content generation and publishing agent, which means I’ve been teaching software how to recognize voice, tone, subject boundaries, and what makes one project sound like itself instead of sounding like everything else online. House Dainislaav is part of that, but the system itself isn’t specific to the House anymore.\r\n\r\nHonestly, this has been one of the more interesting problems in the whole build. It’s not that hard to make a machine produce words. It’s much harder to make it stay in character without drifting into generic sludge, fake profundity, or “hello fellow humans, I have done a content.”\r\n\r\nThat part matters to me more than the automation.\r\n\r\nBecause making content faster is useful. Making everything sound the same is not.\r\n\r\nSo yes, I built the weird thing. It’s real, it works, and it has officially crossed the line from “small helper tool” into “this is becoming an actual platform, isn’t it?”\r\n\r\nIf you build software, write music, or do any kind of creative work, you probably already know how this happens. One practical idea puts on armor, picks up a sword, and comes back three weeks later demanding architecture.','published','2026-08-14 16:37:13','2026-08-14 16:37:35','2026-08-14 16:37:13','2026-08-14 16:37:35','997458080122297_122122861874922230',1,NULL),(45,'creator_developer','so, i now have a fully brand aware content generator\r\nFoundry Herald: a multi-brand content generation and publishing agent.  do not mention AI','auto','Built a thing I’ve wanted for a while: Foundry Herald is now fully brand-aware.\r\n\r\nThe practical version of that sentence is this: it can generate content without flattening everything into the same voice. Different projects need different language, different priorities, different boundaries, and different kinds of posts. A music-development platform should not sound like an artist page. A reflective post should not sound like a feature update. A build log should not read like promo copy.\r\n\r\nGetting that right was a lot less about “write post” and a lot more about context, rules, voice separation, topic control, and teaching the system what not to do. Which, unsurprisingly, is where the real work lives.\r\n\r\nThis is one of those creator/developer crossover moments I love. On the surface, it’s a content tool. Underneath, it’s really about creative identity staying intact when the workflow scales.\r\n\r\nIf you’ve ever built for more than one project at once, you know the problem. The hard part usually isn’t generating more words. It’s keeping each thing recognizably itself.','published','2026-08-14 16:39:26','2026-08-14 16:39:34','2026-08-14 16:39:26','2026-08-14 16:39:34','1237870646072318_122112575913396744',2,NULL),(46,'song_promotion','Promotion for the new album, \"Ashes of Tomorrow\"  Listen to it on Spotify, https://open.spotify.com/album/2g0HTP0d3Q2eoY3Aq6KEvF?si=l_JT6eUXQ9qlxj_VNTF54Q','auto','Ashes of Tomorrow is out.\r\n\r\nThis album came from a place I know well by now: the stretch of road where things have already burned down, but the story is not over. Not triumph. Not clean resolution. More like smoke in the lungs, grit in the teeth, and the decision to keep moving anyway.\r\n\r\nThat’s the heart of House Dainislaav for me. Not pretending the fire didn’t happen. Not acting invincible. Becoming something stronger because it did.\r\n\r\nIf you’ve been following the music, the voice, the banners, the forge, the heavier songs and the more reflective ones, this album carries a lot of that blood in it.\r\n\r\nYou can listen to Ashes of Tomorrow on Spotify here:\r\nhttps://open.spotify.com/album/2g0HTP0d3Q2eoY3Aq6KEvF?si=l_JT6eUXQ9qlxj_VNTF54Q\r\n\r\nIf you give it a listen, I’d genuinely like to know which track hits you first.','published','2026-08-14 19:29:06','2026-08-14 19:29:24','2026-08-14 19:29:06','2026-08-14 19:29:24','997458080122297_122122870058922230',1,NULL),(47,'engagement','regarding the music i post','auto','Quick question for the people following House Dainislaav for the music:\r\n\r\nWhat do you want more of from the songs I post here?\r\n\r\nShort clips, full tracks, lyric-focused posts, behind-the-song breakdowns, videos, heavier pieces, quieter atmospheric stuff, or something else entirely?\r\n\r\nI’m genuinely curious what connects with you most.','published','2026-08-15 08:12:17','2026-08-15 08:12:28','2026-08-15 08:12:17','2026-08-15 08:12:28','997458080122297_122122931468922230',1,NULL),(48,'engagement','regarding what went in to building the platform','auto','Building a creative platform is a weird mix of music questions and software questions.\r\n\r\nSome days it’s architecture, state, workflow logic, and figuring out how to carry decisions forward without the whole thing turning into a pile of disconnected prompts. Other days it’s much more basic: what does an artist actually need at the moment they’re stuck? Not what sounds impressive in a feature list. What actually helps.\r\n\r\nA lot of the work has gone into that middle layer between “I have an idea” and “I have something usable.” Translating instinct into direction. Keeping identity consistent across songs. Making the system useful for people who don’t speak fluent producer, prompt engineer, or developer.\r\n\r\nThat part has been more interesting than just wiring up features.\r\n\r\nIf you were building a platform for songwriting and music development, what would you obsess over first: creative workflow, sonic consistency, ease of use, or something else entirely?','approved','2026-08-15 08:13:55','2026-08-15 08:14:12','2026-08-15 08:13:55',NULL,NULL,2,'Error validating access token: Session has expired on Friday, 14-Aug-26 14:00:00 PDT. The current time is Saturday, 15-Aug-26 05:14:05 PDT.'),(49,'auto','regarding the following quote:  \"Every setback asks one question: continue?\"','auto','I like this line because it’s blunt.\r\n\r\n“Every setback asks one question: continue?”\r\n\r\nNot “Are you inspired?”\r\nNot “Do you still feel powerful?”\r\nNot “Did the universe send a sign?”\r\n\r\nJust: continue?\r\n\r\nA lot of the hard moments in creative work, music, building anything, or rebuilding yourself don’t arrive with drums behind them. Usually it’s more ordinary than that. Something fails. Something stalls. You get ignored. You realize the version in your head is better than what your hands can make today.\r\n\r\nAnd then you get the question.\r\n\r\nNot once, either. Over and over.\r\n\r\nI’ve been thinking lately that persistence is less glamorous than people make it sound. Most of the time it’s not heroic. It’s just deciding, again, that the work is still worth doing.\r\n\r\nThat question shows up for everybody eventually. The answer is where things start to get take shape.','approved','2026-08-15 08:22:19','2026-08-15 08:22:19','2026-08-15 08:22:19',NULL,NULL,1,NULL),(50,'project_spotlight','Sonic Foundry.  Why did i create it','auto','I built Sonic Foundry because I wanted a better way to turn musical ideas into actual songs than “type one big prompt and hope.”\r\n\r\nWhat interested me wasn’t just generating output. It was the process. Real songwriting usually happens through a series of decisions: mood, structure, lyrical direction, arrangement, revision, and all the small choices that shape the end result. I wanted to build something that respected that workflow instead of flattening it into a single text box.\r\n\r\nThat became the core idea behind Sonic Foundry: software that helps guide creative decisions step by step, with context carried through the process, instead of treating every request like it starts from zero.\r\n\r\nIt’s also the kind of project I like building because it sits right at the intersection of product thinking and engineering. Database-backed context, API integration, workflow design, state, persistence, and figuring out how to make the interface support the creative process instead of getting in its way.\r\n\r\nIn other words, I didn’t create it because I needed another demo project.\r\n\r\nI created it because I thought the workflow itself deserved better software.','approved','2026-08-15 09:42:42','2026-08-15 09:42:42','2026-08-15 09:42:42',NULL,NULL,3,NULL),(51,'technical_lesson','AI + WordPress series, Post 1 of 7:\r\nAI in WordPress is more than a chatbot.\r\nIntroduce the series and explain that the better question is not\r\n\"Where can I put a chatbot?\" but \"What useful capability could an LLM add to this application?\"','auto','When AI comes up in WordPress conversations, the first idea is usually a chatbot.\r\n\r\nPut a bubble in the corner, wire it to an API, call it innovation.\r\n\r\nThat can be useful sometimes, but it’s also a pretty narrow way to think about it.\r\n\r\nWhat’s more interesting to me is using an LLM as one capability inside a larger WordPress application. WordPress already knows a lot about structure and state. It knows users, roles, permissions, workflows, content types, metadata, and the rest of the application rules we’ve spent years building around.\r\n\r\nThat means the real question usually isn’t, “Where can I put a chatbot?”\r\n\r\nIt’s, “What useful capability could an LLM add to this application?”\r\n\r\nMaybe that capability is drafting something. Maybe it’s summarizing selected records. Maybe it’s helping classify, transform, or analyze content inside a workflow that WordPress already controls.\r\n\r\nThat distinction matters.\r\n\r\nIf WordPress stays responsible for the application and the model handles a specific task, you get something a lot more useful than a generic chat interface glued onto a site.\r\n\r\nThat’s the thread I’m exploring in this series: practical AI + WordPress work without turning the whole thing into a ChatGPT wrapper.','approved','2026-08-15 14:19:26','2026-08-15 14:19:26','2026-08-15 14:19:26',NULL,NULL,3,NULL);
